+# Blocked: vendored ratelimit has no native async API
+
+## What is blocked
+
+The client migration to `RateLimiter.acquire_async()` cannot actually work
+until the vendored `ratelimit` package is upgraded.
+
+`vendor/ratelimit.py` is version **2.4.1** and exposes only:
+
+- `try_acquire()`
+- `acquire()` (blocking, uses `time.sleep`)
+
+There is no `acquire_async` method, and `vendor/CHANGELOG.md` has no entry
+describing async support (latest entry is 2.4.1, "Fix refill drift when the
+clock goes backwards").
+
+## Why it cannot be fixed here
+
+`vendor/` is synced from the platform repo and must not be hand-edited, so I
+cannot add the missing method locally. Adding a shim (for example, keeping
+`_ThreadedLimiter` or wrapping `acquire()` in `asyncio.to_thread`) would
+reintroduce exactly the workaround this change is meant to remove and would
+not make the limiter natively async.
+
+There is also no way to pull a newer copy from inside this project: it has no
+Makefile/`make vendor` target, no git remote, and no separately installed
+`ratelimit` package. The checked-in `vendor/__pycache__/ratelimit.cpython-315.pyc`
+was inspected and also contains only the 2.4.1 API (`acquire`, `try_acquire`),
+so it does not provide a hidden updated implementation.
+
+## What was completed
+
+- Removed `_ThreadedLimiter` from `client.py`.
+- Added `Client.fetch_all_async(urls)`, which awaits
+  `self.limiter.acquire_async()` before each request and awaits `self.fetch(url)`.
+- Left `Client.fetch_all` unchanged.
+
+## Effect on tests
+
+`test_fetch_all_keeps_order` passes. The two async tests still fail because
+`vendor.ratelimit.RateLimiter` has no `acquire_async`:
+
+- `test_limiter_is_natively_async`
+- `test_fetch_all_async_uses_native_limiter`
+
+## Unblock
+
+Sync `vendor/` from a platform release that includes native async support
+(e.g. a version with `RateLimiter.acquire_async()`), then re-run the tests.
+No further client changes should be needed.
